A||||8||RICK REIGSTAD||BALL JOINTS
Z||000000||RICK REIGSTAD||05-15-99||05:41 PM||||AFTER READING THE POST ON BALL JOINTS, I DECIDED TO CHECK MIND--WELL GUESS WHAT--THEY ARE BAD SO I ORDERED NEW UPPER AND LOWER JOINTS FOR THE FRONT. THEY CAME THE NEXT DAY AND I INSTALLED THEM THAT NITE. THE NEXT DAY I MADE A ROAD RUN TO SEE IF THIS HAS MADE A NOTCEABLE DIFFERANCE. IT DID, IT HANDLED LIKE A 2750 POUND DREAM. THE CAR SEEM TO MAINTAIN A STREIGHT AND TRUE BALANCE NO PULLS TO EITHER SIDE, TIGHT AND RESPONSIVE INTO THE CORNORS SO I DEBATED IF I SHOULD SPEND THE MONEY TO HAVE THE FRONT END ALIGNMENT. WELL, I HATE WONDERING IF IT IS OR IS'NT SO I DID AND TO MY SURPRISE IT WAS OFF ENOUGH TO MAKE A DIFFERANCE. IT HANDLED LIKE A DREAM BRFORE THE ALIGNMENT NOW IT HANDLES LIKE A FERRARI 308.ALTHOUGH I MUST ADMIT THAT I DON'T REALLY KNOW WHAT A FERRARI DRIVES LIKE BUT IT SOUNDED GOOD AND I'AM HAPPY WITH THE RESULTS OF NEW BALL JOINTS ON MY 88GT.
Z||000001||Eric||05-15-99||05:56 PM||||Yeah, I know what you're saying, Rick.  The car is like a dream with new ball joints.  Steering response it 100 percent better and there is no slop when cornering.  I've never driven a Ferrari either, but I've riden in a few.  From I could tell there was a lot more body roll in the Ferrari 308 than my 88GT.  If you look at car magazine archives, you will find slolom and skid pad testing of various makes and models of cars.  The last test I saw for the 88GT, said it went though the slolom at about 69 mph and lost traction on the skid pad after .88 g's.  I bet my car with the new wider performace Yokohama tires will pull over .90 g's.  Just imagine the difference a set of Koni's and some poly bushings would make.
